===Pronoun===
{{etymid|ru|pronoun}}
{{head|ru|pronoun|head=э́тот|g=m|demonstrative||feminine|э́та|neuter|э́то|plural|э́ти}}

# [[this]]/[[that]] [[one]], [[these]]/[[those]] ([[ones]])
#: {{syn|ru|да́нный<q:higher register, partial>|сей<q:dated or poetic>|тот<q:partial><id:pronoun>}}
#: {{ux|ru|'''Э́та''' [[моде́ль]] [[сто́ит]] [[пятьсо́т]] [[рубле́й]], [[а]] [[вот]] '''э́та''' [[сто́ит]] [[семьсо́т]].|subst=моде́ль//модэ́ль|This model costs five hundred roubles, and '''this one''' costs seven hundred.|footer=(''Translated as "this one" / "these (ones)" when indicating something physically or mentally proximal to the speaker.'')}}
#: {{ux|ru|'''Э́тот''' [[я́ркий]] [[объе́кт]] - [[Вене́ра]], [[а]] [[вот]] '''э́тот''' - [[Си́риус]].|That bright object is Venus, and '''that one''' is Sirius.|footer=(''Translated as "that one" / "those (ones)" when indicating something physically or mentally distal to the speaker.'')}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[мне|Мне]] [[нра́вятся]] [[мно́гие]] [[её]] [[рису́нки]], [[а]] [[вот]] '''э́ти''' - [[не]] [[о́чень]].|I like many of her drawings, but '''these (ones)''' not so much.}}

====Usage notes====
* See {{m|ru|э́то|id=pronoun}} for additional pronoun senses specific to the neuter singular.
* {{anchor|Russian:_overlap}} Although in theory proximal, use of {{m-self|ru|э́тот}} overlaps with that of the English distal demonstrative pronouns, ''[[that]]'' and ''[[those]]''. The theoretically distal Russian demonstrative pronoun, {{m|ru|тот|id=pronoun}}, is mostly used in a few mostly grammatically supportive roles.
* Due to often usage in colloquial speech, {{m-self|ru|э́тот}} is considered inappropriate for documents and formal letters. The [[higher register]] terms {{m|ru|да́нный|id=pronoun}}, {{m|ru|настоя́щий|id=pronoun}}, {{m|ru|вышеука́занный|id=pronoun}} and {{m|ru|нижеука́занный|id=pronoun}} are used instead.

